window.__hvBdRegister("livy-4", {"key":"livy-4","sections":[{"i":0,"j":12,"loc":"Book XXXI 1–9","title":"Rome votes for war against Philip","summary":"Livy opens the book by reflecting on the growing size of his work, then turns from the Punic war to the war with Macedonia, which the Athenians, Attalus and the Rhodians urge on Rome. Embassies go to Ptolemy, and Laevinus reports Philip's large preparations. When the consul Sulpicius proposes war, the assembly first rejects it, wearied by the long Punic struggle and stirred by the tribune Baebius. Sulpicius argues that the choice is not peace or war but whether to fight in Macedonia or in Italy, recalling Saguntum and Pyrrhus. The people then vote for war, and armies, vows and games are arranged.","ideas":["The people at first refuse a new war out of exhaustion","Sulpicius: fight Philip abroad or face him in Italy","Saguntum and Pyrrhus used as warnings against delay","A vow of games is made for an unspecified sum for the first time"],"terms":[{"t":"fetial declaration","d":"The heralds advise that war may be declared to Philip in person or at his nearest garrison."},{"t":"comitia centuriata","d":"The assembly by centuries in the Field of Mars, which first rejects and then approves the war."}]},{"i":13,"j":16,"loc":"Book XXXI 10–13","title":"Gallic rising and business at Rome","summary":"The Insubrians, Boians and Ligurians under the Carthaginian Hamilcar sack Placentia and besiege Cremona, and the praetor Furius reports he is too weak to relieve it. The senate orders the consul's army to Ariminum and sends envoys to Carthage demanding Hamilcar's surrender, and to Masinissa with gifts and a request for Numidian cavalry. Vermina, son of Syphax, is told he must sue for peace before being called king. A theft from Proserpine's treasury at Locri and many prodigies are expiated, and public creditors are paid in public land instead of money.","ideas":["Hamilcar leads Gauls against the Roman colonies","Rome demands Carthage recall and surrender Hamilcar","Prodigies, including hermaphrodites, are expiated by rites","Creditors receive public land near Rome in lieu of payment"],"terms":[{"t":"decemvirs (of the sacred books)","d":"Priests who consult the Sibylline books and prescribe the expiation of prodigies."},{"t":"Trientius and Tabulius land","d":"Public land granted to creditors in place of the third installment of their loan."}]},{"i":17,"j":23,"loc":"Book XXXI 14–20","title":"Athens joins the war; the fall of Abydus","summary":"Sulpicius crosses to Macedonia and sends Claudius Centho to relieve Athens. Livy traces the Athenian quarrel to the execution of two uninitiated Acarnanians at the mysteries, and describes the honours Athens pays Attalus and the Rhodians as it declares war. Philip seizes towns in Thrace and the Chersonesus and besieges Abydus, whose people vow to kill their families rather than surrender and then do so. The Roman envoy Aemilius confronts Philip, who answers defiantly. Carthage and Masinissa send grain and cavalry, Vermina accepts terms, and Lentulus is granted an ovation for Spain.","ideas":["The Athenian war began from a trivial sacrilege case","Attalus and the Rhodians fail to press Philip hard","The mass self-destruction of the people of Abydus","Aemilius' blunt challenge to Philip"],"terms":[{"t":"ovation","d":"A lesser triumph, granted to Lentulus because he held Spain only as proconsul."}]},{"i":24,"j":29,"loc":"Book XXXI 21–26","title":"Cremona, Chalcis, and Philip in Attica","summary":"Furius defeats the Gauls before Cremona, killing Hamilcar and over thirty-five thousand men. In Greece, Claudius Centho surprises and burns Chalcis, freeing prisoners and destroying the king's stores. Philip rushes to avenge it, attacks Athens at the Dipylon gate, fights in person, and then burns Cynosarges, the Lyceum and tombs. At the Achaean council at Argos he offers to fight Nabis in exchange for troops, but the praetor Cycliades blocks him. Philip and Philocles then fail at Eleusis and Piraeus, and Philip destroys the temples of the Attic countryside.","ideas":["Furius' victory over the Gauls and death of Hamilcar","The surprise raid on Chalcis and the Euripus as Greece's sea-gate","Philip's charge at the Dipylon and burning of Attic shrines","The Achaeans see through Philip's request for troops"],"terms":[{"t":"hemerodromoi (day-runners)","d":"Greek couriers who cover a long journey in a day; one warns Athens of Philip's approach."}]},{"i":30,"j":35,"loc":"Book XXXI 27–32","title":"First raids and the Aetolian council","summary":"The legate Apustius raids the Macedonian frontier, destroying Antipatria. Pleuratus, Amynander and the Dardanians offer aid, while Philip sends Perseus to hold the pass at Pelagonia. At the Panaetolian assembly the Macedonian envoy warns that Rome enslaves those it claims to free, citing Sicily, Rhegium and Capua, and that Greeks are by nature at war with barbarians. The Athenians denounce Philip's impiety against tombs and temples, and the Roman envoy Furius defends Roman conduct and invites Aetolia back into alliance. The praetor Damocritus has the decision postponed so Aetolia can side with the winner.","ideas":["Macedonian speech: Romans are foreign masters","Athenian speech: Philip warred on gods above and below","Roman reply defends Rhegium, Syracuse and Capua","Damocritus defers the vote to wait on fortune"],"terms":[{"t":"Panaetolium","d":"The general assembly of the Aetolians, which alone could decide on war or peace."}]},{"i":36,"j":43,"loc":"Book XXXI 33–40","title":"Sulpicius and Philip in western Macedonia","summary":"Sulpicius marches through Dassaretia while Philip prepares. A cavalry skirmish leads Philip to display the dead, and his men are frightened by wounds from the Spanish sword. Philip recalls Perseus from Pelagonia, admires the Roman camp, and loses cavalry engagements, nearly being killed near Ortholophus when his horse falls. Livy weighs criticisms of both commanders. Philip slips away under a truce, fortifies the defiles, but the Romans force them and ravage Eordaea and Orestis, taking Celetrum and Pelium, while the Aetolians are drawn back to Rome's side.","ideas":["Macedonians horrified by the Spanish sword","Philip judges the Roman camp not that of barbarians","Philip's narrow escape after his horse falls","The phalanx is useless in wooded defiles"],"terms":[{"t":"phalanx","d":"The Macedonian close formation with very long spears, needing open ground to be effective."},{"t":"testudo (tortoise)","d":"Roman formation of overlapping shields used to force the passes and approach Celetrum."}]},{"i":44,"j":49,"loc":"Book XXXI 41–46","title":"Aetolian rout and the allied fleet","summary":"The Aetolians and Amynander plunder Thessaly carelessly until Philip surprises them near Phecadus; their camp is taken and Amynander guides them home. Athenagoras harasses the retreating Dardanians, and Scopas hires away Aetolian troops for Egypt. Athens passes decrees cursing Philip and erasing all honours to him and his ancestors. The Roman fleet under Apustius with Attalus takes Andros, fails at Cythnus and Cassandrea, sacks Acanthus, and after a hard siege captures Oreus in Euboea.","ideas":["Philip routs the undisciplined Aetolians","Athens wages war by decrees and curses","Andros given to Attalus, spoils kept by Rome","Siege and capture of Oreus"],"terms":[]},{"i":50,"j":54,"loc":"Book XXXI 47–50","title":"Furius' disputed triumph and the elections","summary":"The fleet withdraws for winter and Attalus returns to Asia after the Eleusinian mysteries. The consul Aurelius, angry that Furius fought in his absence, sends him to Etruria, but Furius goes to Rome and asks for a triumph. Senior senators object that the army was the consul's, while the majority hold that the victory was won under his own auspices, and he triumphs without prisoners, spoils or soldiers. Aurelius complains that no witness of the battle was heard. Consuls and praetors are elected, veterans receive land, and the aediles' games and a flamen's oath are arranged.","ideas":["Debate on whether a praetor may triumph with a consul's army","Furius triumphs without soldiers or captives","Aurelius objects that the triumph rests on one man's word","A substitute swears the oath for the flamen Dialis"],"terms":[{"t":"auspices","d":"The right to take the omens, which determined under whose command a victory was won."},{"t":"flamen Dialis","d":"Priest of Jupiter, barred from swearing the oath to the laws."}]},{"i":55,"j":65,"loc":"Book XXXII 1–7","title":"Villius' year and Flamininus elected","summary":"Villius takes Macedonia and Lentulus Italy; prodigies are expiated and Carthage pays tribute in debased silver. Villius quiets a mutiny of veterans demanding discharge. Philip fails at Thaumaci, wins back the Achaeans with restored towns, imprisons the unpopular Heraclides, and fortifies the Aous gorge. Villius debates how to attack until news comes that Titus Quinctius has been chosen consul; Livy rejects Valerius Antias' report of a great victory by Villius. At Rome, Scipio and Paetus are censors, Baebius loses an army to the Insubrians, and tribunes fail to block Flamininus' candidacy straight from the quaestorship.","ideas":["Mutiny of the veterans brought from Africa","Philip's fortified position on the Aous","Livy doubts Valerius Antias' battle account","Flamininus reaches the consulship without the usual offices"],"terms":[{"t":"Stena","d":"The narrow pass at Antigonia on the Aous, which Philip occupies."}]},{"i":66,"j":70,"loc":"Book XXXII 8–12","title":"Flamininus forces the Aous gorge","summary":"Macedonia falls to Titus Quinctius; the senate tells Attalus' envoys it will ask Antiochus to leave his kingdom alone. Quinctius crosses early and decides to attack Philip directly. A conference on the Aous breaks down when Quinctius demands freedom for Thessaly and Philip asks what worse he could impose on a defeated king. A herdsman sent by Charopus guides a detachment over the mountains above the king's camp. Attacked from front and rear, the Macedonians flee, Philip losing about two thousand men and his camp.","ideas":["Rome uses allied aid only as the allies wish","Philip refuses to give up Thessaly","The herdsman's path over the heights","Smoke signal and the rout at the Aous"],"terms":[]},{"i":71,"j":76,"loc":"Book XXXII 13–18","title":"Thessaly, Euboea, and the siege of Atrax","summary":"Philip retreats, burning allied Thessalian towns so the enemy cannot use them, while the Aetolians and Amynander plunder Thessaly. Quinctius conciliates the Epirots, sacks Phaloria and moves to Atrax. Lucius Quinctius with Attalus and the Rhodians takes Eretria, rich in old statues and pictures, and Carystus. At Atrax the Macedonian phalanx holds the breach against Roman cohorts and a leaning siege tower. Quinctius raises the siege, winters at Anticyra, and takes towns in Phocis before Elatia.","ideas":["Thessaly ravaged by three armies at once","Capture of Eretria and Carystus","The phalanx defends the breach at Atrax","Anticyra chosen as winter base"],"terms":[{"t":"phalanx","d":"At Atrax its hedge of long spears, even broken ones, keeps out the Roman swordsmen."}]},{"i":77,"j":83,"loc":"Book XXXII 19–25","title":"The Achaeans join Rome; Corinth and Argos","summary":"Envoys of Rome, Attalus, Rhodes, Philip and Athens address the Achaean council at Sicyon, offering Corinth if the league changes sides. When the assembly sits silent, the praetor Aristaenus argues that Philip cannot protect them, that neutrality is no middle way, and that they must have Rome as ally or enemy. After a bitter dispute among the demiurgi, the league allies with Attalus and Rhodes and refers the Roman alliance to Rome, while Dymae, Megalopolis and some Argives withdraw. The siege of Corinth fails, Quinctius takes Elatia, and Argos is betrayed to Philocles, the Achaean Aenesidemus dying in its defence.","ideas":["Aristaenus: neutrality makes one the conqueror's prey","A father threatens his son to force the vote","Failed allied siege of Corinth","Argos restores Philip's name and goes over to him"],"terms":[{"t":"demiurgi","d":"The ten Achaean magistrates who decide whether a question may be put to the council."}]},{"i":84,"j":89,"loc":"Book XXXII 26–31","title":"Slave conspiracy and victories in Gaul","summary":"Aelius achieves little in Gaul, but a slave plot centred on the Carthaginian hostages at Setia is betrayed and crushed by the praetor Merula, with further executions at Praeneste. Attalus sends a golden crown, Cato governs Sardinia sternly and expels usurers, and six praetors are elected for the first time. Tribunes argue that consuls are recalled from Macedonia too soon, so Quinctius' command is extended and both consuls go to Gaul. Cornelius defeats the Insubrians at the Mincius after the Cenomanians stay aloof, and Minucius forces the Boians and Ligurians to submit.","ideas":["Slave rising planned for the games at Setia","Cato's severity against usury in Sardinia","Six praetors for the enlarged empire","Flamininus' command continued to finish the war"],"terms":[{"t":"prorogation","d":"Extension of a magistrate's command beyond his year, granted to Quinctius."}]},{"i":90,"j":99,"loc":"Book XXXII 32–40","title":"Conference at Nicaea and Nabis at Argos","summary":"Philip asks for talks and meets Quinctius and the allies on the shore near Nicaea, refusing to land. Quinctius demands withdrawal from all Greek cities, and the allies add their claims; Philip mocks Phaeneas and offers partial concessions in private, which satisfy no one. A two-month truce lets envoys go to Rome, where the allies show that Demetrias, Chalcis and Corinth are the fetters of Greece, and the senate leaves the decision to Quinctius. Philip hands Argos to Nabis, who plunders it and proposes cancelling debts and dividing land. Nabis meets Quinctius and Attalus, gives Cretan troops and a truce, and his wife strips the Argive women.","ideas":["Philip will not leave his ship for the conference","Demetrias, Chalcis and Corinth as the fetters of Greece","Quinctius given full power over war and peace","Nabis seizes Argos and proposes debt cancellation and land division"],"terms":[{"t":"fetters of Greece","d":"Philip's own phrase for the three fortresses whose possession kept Greece unfree."},{"t":"tyrant","d":"Nabis of Lacedaemon, ruling by force and plundering the Argives."}]},{"i":100,"j":108,"loc":"Book XXXIII 1–5","title":"Boeotia joins Rome; armies gather in Thessaly","summary":"Book XXXIII opens with Quinctius marching on Thebes, where he slips his spearmen into the city behind a friendly escort, so the Boeotian assembly cannot refuse alliance with Rome; Attalus collapses mid-speech with a stroke. Philip, finding Macedonia drained of young men, enlists boys of sixteen and veterans and drills his army at Dius. Quinctius gathers Aetolian, Cretan, Apollonian and Athamanian auxiliaries, and Philip rallies his troops by blaming earlier defeats on guards and light troops, not the phalanx. After a failed attempt on Phthiotic Thebes, Livy contrasts clumsy Greek palisades with the light, interlaced Roman stakes.","ideas":["Boeotian alliance obtained under the quiet threat of hidden troops","Macedonian manpower exhausted by generations of war","Philip's faith in the phalanx on level ground","Roman palisade technique compared with the Greek"],"terms":[{"t":"phalanx","d":"The dense Macedonian heavy-infantry formation with long spears, here sixteen thousand strong and the core of Philip's army."},{"t":"peltastae (targeteers)","d":"Lighter shield-bearing infantry serving alongside the Macedonian phalanx."}]},{"i":109,"j":114,"loc":"Book XXXIII 6–10","title":"The battle of Cynoscephalae","summary":"The two armies manoeuvre near Pherae and Scotussa, hidden from each other by hills, until fog and rain set outposts clashing on the ridges called Cynoscephalae. Reinforcements are fed in on both sides; Philip, told the Romans are fleeing, reluctantly commits his whole force on ground and at a time he dislikes. His right wing wins on the heights, but Quinctius drives elephants into the unformed left, and a tribune with twenty companies takes the victorious Macedonian right in the rear, so the phalanx, unable to turn, is cut down. Philip flees to Tempe; Livy follows Polybius's figures of eight thousand killed and five thousand taken, rejecting Valerius Antias's inflated totals.","ideas":["Fog turns a chance skirmish into a general battle","Phalanx strong in front but helpless when attacked from the rear","An unnamed tribune's initiative decides the day","Livy prefers Polybius to Valerius Antias on numbers"],"terms":[{"t":"Cynoscephalae","d":"The hills in Thessaly where Philip V was decisively defeated by Quinctius."}]},{"i":115,"j":121,"loc":"Book XXXIII 11–17","title":"Peace talks with Philip; Corinth and Leucas","summary":"Philip burns his records at Larissa and asks for a truce; Quinctius's mild message offends the Aetolians, whose greed and claims to the victory he in turn resents and works to diminish. In council the Aetolians demand Philip's death or exile, but Quinctius cites the Roman habit of sparing the vanquished and the value of Macedonia as a barrier against Thracians, Illyrians and Gauls. At Tempe Philip accepts all terms; a dispute over Phthiotic Thebes deepens the Aetolian grievance, and a four-month truce is set, partly because Antiochus threatens Europe. Meanwhile the Achaeans under Nicostratus rout Androsthenes near Corinth, and Lucius Quinctius storms Leucas, after which Acarnania submits.","ideas":["Quinctius refuses to destroy Macedonia, citing clemency and policy","Aetolian resentment sown over the spoils of victory","Philip yields everything to win peace quickly","Achaean surprise victory and the fall of Leucas"],"terms":[{"t":"Aetolians","d":"Rome's Greek allies in the war, whose claim to credit and to captured towns Quinctius resists."}]},{"i":122,"j":129,"loc":"Book XXXIII 18–26","title":"Rhodes, Antiochus' advance, and affairs at Rome","summary":"The Rhodians beat Philip's general Dinocrates near Alabanda but waste the chance to take Stratonice. Philip throws back a Dardanian invasion near Stobi, while Antiochus sails along Cilicia and Caria; the Rhodians boldly warn him off and, on news of Cynoscephalae, protect cities allied to Ptolemy. Attalus dies, praised for using wealth to earn a throne, and revolt breaks out in Farther Spain. At Rome tribunes force the consuls to seek triumphs separately (Minucius triumphs on the Alban mount), the people vote unanimously for peace with Philip, and news comes of Tuditanus' defeat and death in Spain, followed by provinces and prodigies.","ideas":["Rhodian courage against Antiochus","Portrait of Attalus of Pergamus","Separate triumphs for unequal merit","The tribes ratify peace with Macedonia"],"terms":[{"t":"triumph on the Alban mount","d":"A triumph a commander held on his own authority and at his own expense when the senate refused one."},{"t":"supplicatio","d":"Public thanksgiving at the temples decreed for victories, here for five days."}]},{"i":130,"j":136,"loc":"Book XXXIII 27–33","title":"Boeotian murders and the Isthmian proclamation","summary":"Boeotians restored by Quinctius thank Philip instead and elect Brachyllas, a Macedonian partisan, as Boeotarch; pro-Roman leaders have him assassinated, and the plot of Zeuxippus and Pisistratus is exposed through a misdelivered letter. Boeotians then murder some five hundred Roman soldiers until Quinctius ravages the country and, through Achaean mediation, imposes a fine. The ten commissioners grant Philip peace: Greek freedom, withdrawal of garrisons, surrender of fleet and a thousand talents; the Aetolians object that Corinth, Chalcis and Demetrias are kept back. At the Isthmian games the herald proclaims the Greeks free, and the crowd, hardly believing it, mobs Quinctius in joy.","ideas":["Factional murder in Boeotia and Roman reprisal","Terms of peace with Philip","Debate over keeping the 'fetters' Corinth, Chalcis, Demetrias against Antiochus","The proclamation of Greek freedom at the Isthmus"],"terms":[{"t":"Boeotarch","d":"Chief magistrate of the Boeotian league."},{"t":"Isthmian games","d":"The great festival at Corinth where the freedom of the Greeks was proclaimed."}]},{"i":137,"j":140,"loc":"Book XXXIII 34–37","title":"Settling Greece; war with the Gauls","summary":"Quinctius orders Antiochus' envoys to leave the cities of Philip and Ptolemy and to keep out of Europe, then the council assigns territories: Thessaly, Magnesia and others are freed, Corinth goes to the Achaeans, and Aetolian claims are referred to the senate. The commissioners disperse; Cornelius advises Philip to seek alliance with Rome and hears Aetolian complaints at the Pylaic assembly. In Italy a slave conspiracy in Etruria is crushed. Marcellus suffers a defeat by the Boians, then beats the Insubrians at Comum; with Furius he devastates the Boians, and both consuls win further successes and Marcellus a triumph.","ideas":["Rome warns Antiochus to stay out of Europe","Redistribution of Philip's former possessions","Aetolian claims that Rome owed them its victory","Mixed fortunes against Boians and Insubrians"],"terms":[{"t":"Pylaic assembly","d":"The regular gathering of Greek states held at Thermopylae."}]},{"i":141,"j":144,"loc":"Book XXXIII 38–41","title":"Antiochus at Lysimachia","summary":"Antiochus besieges Smyrna and Lampsacus, which claim independence, and crosses to the Chersonese, where he begins rebuilding ruined Lysimachia. Roman envoys meet him there and demand that he restore Ptolemy's cities and keep out of Europe. Antiochus retorts that Asia is no concern of Rome's and that he is reclaiming the lands of Lysimachus, won by Seleucus by right of conquest. A false rumour of Ptolemy's death breaks off talks; Antiochus sails for Egypt, learns Ptolemy lives, loses much of his fleet in a storm off Sarus, and winters at Antioch.","ideas":["Smyrna and Lampsacus resist Antiochus","Rebuilding of Lysimachia as a royal seat","Rival claims: Roman protection versus Seleucid inheritance","Storm wrecks Antiochus' fleet"],"terms":[]},{"i":145,"j":154,"loc":"Book XXXIII 42–49","title":"Rome's new year and Hannibal's flight","summary":"The triumviri epulones are created, priests are made to pay arrears of war-tax, and Valerius Flaccus and Marcus Porcius Cato are elected consuls; Spain falls to Cato. The ten commissioners report that Antiochus and Nabis of Sparta are the coming dangers, and the senate leaves Nabis to Quinctius. At Carthage Hannibal as praetor broke the life-tenure of the judges and exposed embezzlement of revenues, making enemies who denounce him at Rome despite Scipio's objection. When Roman envoys arrive he escapes by night to his coast estate and Cercina, where a feast hides his departure, and reaches Antiochus at Ephesus, spurring him toward war.","ideas":["Antiochus and Nabis named as the next threats","Hannibal's reforms of the Carthaginian judges and finances","Scipio opposes Rome's pursuit of Hannibal","Hannibal's flight to Antiochus"],"terms":[{"t":"triumviri epulones","d":"A new priestly board of three created to manage sacred feasts."},{"t":"order of judges (Carthage)","d":"A life-tenured Carthaginian court whose power Hannibal limited to annual terms."}]},{"i":155,"j":166,"loc":"Book XXXIV 1–8","title":"The debate on the Oppian law","summary":"Book XXXIV opens with tribunes proposing to repeal the Oppian law, a wartime limit on women's gold, coloured dress and carriages, and matrons crowd the streets to lobby for it. Cato argues that women's public action threatens husbands' authority, that repeal weakens all law, and that luxury and avarice, imported from Greece and Asia, ruin empires. Lucius Valerius replies that matrons have often acted publicly for the city, that laws made for a crisis are temporary, and that women, denied offices and triumphs, deserve their own marks of distinction. The women besiege the opposing tribunes, the law is repealed, and Cato sails for Spain.","ideas":["Cato: female assembly as sedition and luxury as ruin","Valerius: emergency laws lapse with the emergency","Historical precedents of matrons acting for Rome","Repeal after the women's pressure on the tribunes"],"terms":[{"t":"lex Oppia","d":"Law passed during the Hannibalic war limiting women's gold, purple dress and carriage use."},{"t":"luxuria (luxury)","d":"Cato's chief moral enemy, paired with avarice as the ruin of great states."}]},{"i":167,"j":173,"loc":"Book XXXIV 9–15","title":"Cato's campaign and victory near Emporiae","summary":"Livy describes Emporiae, a Greek town kept safe beside a Spanish one by strict watchfulness. Cato sends the grain buyers home, saying the war will feed itself, and Helvius and Minucius return from Spain with ovation and triumph. Unable to spare troops for the Ilergetian chief Bilistages, Cato embarks a third of his men to create the appearance of relief, then recalls them. He trains his army by raids, marches by night behind the enemy camp so his men have no refuge but courage, and wins a hard battle by flanking cohorts, fresh reserves and an assault on the camp.","ideas":["Discipline and vigilance of Greek Emporiae","Appearances in war can work like real forces","Cato cuts off retreat to force his men to fight","Use of reserves and a fresh legion to take the camp"],"terms":[{"t":"ovation","d":"A lesser triumph, granted to Helvius because he fought under another's auspices."}]},{"i":174,"j":179,"loc":"Book XXXIV 16–21","title":"Cato subdues Hither Spain","summary":"Livy praises three features of Cato's generalship; submissions follow, and rebel Bergistans are sold into slavery after a second revolt. Manlius beats the Turditanians, and Cato, fearing more revolts, disarms Spain north of the Ebro and razes the town walls in a single day when the chiefs give no counsel. Livy notes that Cato had to impose submission on free peoples and shared his soldiers' hardships. He offers hired Celtiberians three choices, takes the Lacetanian town by a feint, captures Vergium through an inside party, and organises the iron and silver mines.","ideas":["Disarmament and demolition of walls to prevent revolt","Cato's personal austerity and energy","Stratagems at the Lacetanian town and Vergium","Mines settled to yield revenue"],"terms":[{"t":"Celtiberians","d":"Mercenaries hired by the Turditanians, offered double pay, safe return home, or battle."}]},{"i":180,"j":184,"loc":"Book XXXIV 22–25","title":"Council at Corinth and war on Nabis","summary":"Valerius Flaccus beats the Boians near the forest of Litana. In Greece, with a decree of war against Nabis, Quinctius asks the Greeks at Corinth whether Argos should remain under the tyrant. The Athenian envoy praises Rome; the Aetolian Alexander attacks Athenians and Achaeans, accuses Rome of keeping garrisons in Chalcis and Demetrias, and offers to remove Nabis themselves. Aristaenus of the Achaeans calls the Aetolians barely human robbers, and the assembly votes for war. As Quinctius approaches Argos, the young Damocles' rash rising against Nabis' garrison commander Pythagoras is crushed and many Argives flee to the Romans.","ideas":["Argos framed as the last piece of Greek liberation","Aetolian charge that Rome stays on pretext","Achaean hostility toward the Aetolians","Failed rising of Damocles at Argos"],"terms":[]},{"i":185,"j":188,"loc":"Book XXXIV 26–29","title":"Invasion of Laconia and siege of Gythium","summary":"Against the allies' wish to besiege Argos, Quinctius, backed by Aristaenus, marches against Lacedaemon itself, gathering Macedonian and Thessalian troops, fleets from Rome, Rhodes and Eumenes, and Spartan exiles including Agesipolis, the rightful king. Nabis brings in Cretans, fortifies the city, and has some eighty leading young men and suspect Helots killed to cow the people. The Romans beat off attacks near the Eurotas and ravage Laconia. Lucius Quinctius besieges the naval base of Gythium, where Gorgopas kills his fellow commander for planning surrender but then surrenders himself.","ideas":["Strike at Sparta, not Argos","Nabis rules by terror","Romans repel sallies and lay waste the countryside","The fall of Gythium"],"terms":[{"t":"Helotes","d":"Laconian serfs bound to the land, some of whom Nabis executed for planning to desert."}]},{"i":189,"j":194,"loc":"Book XXXIV 30–35","title":"Conference with Nabis and terms of peace","summary":"Nabis meets Quinctius and defends himself: Argos came to him voluntarily and was left to him by treaty, Quinctius once called him king, and freeing slaves and sharing land follow Spartan tradition of equality. Quinctius answers that Rome's treaty was with Pelops, not a usurping tyrant, and charges Nabis with massacres, the seizure of Messene, alliance with Philip and piracy off Malea. Aristaenus urges him to abdicate. The allies want war to the end, but Quinctius, fearing a long siege, Antiochus and a successor stealing his glory, persuades them through the costs, then dictates terms: Argos evacuated, fleet surrendered, hostages and an indemnity.","ideas":["Nabis contrasts Spartan equality with Roman rule by property","Quinctius: no friendship with a tyrant","Quinctius's hidden motive of personal glory","Detailed peace conditions"],"terms":[{"t":"tyrannus (tyrant)","d":"The title Rome now applied to Nabis, who points out he was earlier addressed as king."}]},{"i":195,"j":199,"loc":"Book XXXIV 36–41","title":"Assault on Sparta and freedom for Argos","summary":"The terms are resented by those holding exiles' wives and property, by freed slaves and by mercenaries; Nabis inflames an assembly with false additions and war resumes. Quinctius assaults the unwalled parts of Sparta with fifty thousand men; the Romans advance under locked shields, but Pythagoras sets houses near the wall alight and the attack is called off. Nabis then sues for peace on the earlier terms. Argos expels its garrison, celebrates the Nemean games with Quinctius presiding, and hears its freedom proclaimed, while Achaeans and Aetolians complain that Sparta was left to the tyrant.","ideas":["Popular anger restarts the war","Fire saves Sparta from capture","Peace on the written terms","Argos freed at the Nemean games"],"terms":[{"t":"testudo (tortoise)","d":"The formation of interlocked shields overhead the Romans used against missiles from rooftops."}]},{"i":200,"j":205,"loc":"Book XXXIV 42–47","title":"Rome under Scipio's second consulship","summary":"Scipio Africanus and Sempronius Longus are elected; peace with Nabis is ratified, and Scipio's argument for keeping Macedonia as a province against Antiochus is overruled, with the armies of Greece and Spain to be disbanded. A defective sacred spring is renewed, the censors seat senators apart at the games, and Pleminius is executed for plotting arson to escape prison. Colonies are founded and prodigies expiated. Valerius defeats the Insubrians and Boians near Mediolanum, Cato triumphs over Spain, and Sempronius fights a bloody, see-saw battle at the gates of his camp against Boiorix's Boians.","ideas":["Scipio warns of Antiochus and Hannibal","Senators given separate seats at the games","Colonies and prodigies","Hard-fought battle with the Boians"],"terms":[{"t":"ver sacrum (sacred spring)","d":"A vow offering the year's spring-born livestock, repeated because it was improperly performed."},{"t":"princeps senatus","d":"Leader of the senate, again named as Scipio."}]},{"i":206,"j":212,"loc":"Book XXXIV 48–54","title":"Quinctius leaves Greece and triumphs","summary":"At Corinth Quinctius reviews Rome's services, defends leaving Nabis weakened rather than destroying Sparta, promises to evacuate Demetrias, Chalcis and Acrocorinth, and urges the Greeks to use liberty with moderation and concord; they weep and ask for Roman captives to be freed, at Achaean cost. He withdraws the garrisons, orders Thessaly by favouring the propertied, and sails home. His three-day triumph displays statues, bullion, coin and crowns, with Demetrius and Nabis' son among the hostages. Latin colonies and temples follow, and the separate seating of senators provokes debate; Scipio is said to have regretted it.","ideas":["Quinctius's farewell advice on liberty and concord","Evacuation of the Greek strongholds","Redeemed Roman captives in the triumph","Resistance to changing ancient custom"],"terms":[]},{"i":213,"j":221,"loc":"Book XXXIV 55–62","title":"Antiochus' embassy, Hannibal's plans, Masinissa","summary":"Constant earthquake reports paralyse public business until a ban on reporting new ones on rite days; a Ligurian rising forces urgent levies. Antiochus' envoy Menippus distinguishes three kinds of treaty and claims an alliance between equals; Quinctius offers two conditions: Antiochus keeps out of Europe or Rome may befriend Asian cities, and he casts Rome as liberator of all Greeks. Carthage reports that Hannibal advises Antiochus to fight in Italy and has sent the Tyrian Aristo, who escapes leaving a tablet implicating the Carthaginian senate. Masinissa seizes the Emporia, and Scipio's commission leaves the boundary dispute undecided.","ideas":["Menippus's three kinds of treaty","Quinctius's choice: Europe or Asia","Hannibal's strategy of war in Italy","Rome deliberately leaves Carthage and Masinissa at odds"],"terms":[{"t":"suffetes","d":"The chief magistrates of Carthage, who found Aristo's tablet."},{"t":"Byrsa","d":"Carthage's original settlement, which the Numidians say was granted as a bull's hide's measure."}]},{"i":222,"j":232,"loc":"Book XXXV 1–7","title":"Spain, Liguria, the Boians, and Roman usury","summary":"After the book's summary, Livy reports mixed fortunes in Spain: Digitius loses half his army, while Publius Cornelius Scipio routs the Lusitanians near Ilipa after vowing games to Jupiter. The senate refuses Flaminius a city legion on the strength of private rumours. In Italy the consul Minucius relieves Pisae from forty thousand Ligurians, and Lucius Cornelius Merula defeats the Boians at Mutina at heavy cost, though his legate Marcus Claudius writes privately blaming him for losses and the enemy's escape. The senate also closes a loophole by which usurers evaded Roman interest laws through allied lenders.","ideas":["Scipio's victory over the Lusitanians near Ilipa","The battle with the Boians at Mutina and the dispute over the consul's conduct","The senate insists on official reports, not rumour","Interest laws extended to loans made through allies and Latins"],"terms":[{"t":"Extraordinaries","d":"Picked allied troops who fought in the front line at Mutina and gave way under the Gallic onset."},{"t":"usury","d":"Lending at excessive interest; here evaded by transferring debts to allies not bound by Roman law."}]},{"i":233,"j":237,"loc":"Book XXXV 8–11","title":"A refused triumph, elections, and a Ligurian trap","summary":"Merula demands a thanksgiving and triumph, but Quintus Metellus objects that his accusing legate has been kept away, and two tribunes threaten a veto. The censors close the lustrum; floods, lightning and other prodigies are expiated, and a Latin colony is planted at Thurii. In a fierce consular contest Lucius Quinctius Flamininus, backed by his brother Titus, defeats Publius Scipio, cousin of Africanus. In Liguria the consul's army is trapped in a defile until Numidian horsemen, feigning clumsiness, slip through and burn villages so that the Ligurians abandon the pass.","ideas":["Triumph blocked pending a hearing of the legate's charges","Flamininus prevails over the Scipios in the elections","Africanus's long fame breeds satiety and envy","The Numidians' ruse breaks the Ligurian blockade"],"terms":[{"t":"lustrum","d":"The purification closing the census; this one counted 143,704 citizens."},{"t":"Caudium","d":"The pass where a Roman army was once trapped, recalled when the consul is hemmed in."}]},{"i":238,"j":244,"loc":"Book XXXV 12–19","title":"Aetolian intrigue and Antiochus's council of war","summary":"The Aetolians, aggrieved at their treatment after the war with Philip, send envoys to stir Nabis, Philip and Antiochus against Rome. Nabis begins tampering with the coastal towns and besieges Gythium. The Roman envoy Villius meets Hannibal at Ephesus, which makes the king suspicious of him; Livy reports, after Acilius, the story of Scipio and Hannibal ranking the greatest generals. At Ephesus Minio and Sulpicius debate whether Antiochus may reclaim the Greek cities of Asia, the talks fail, and the king's council presses for war. Hannibal, excluded, tells the king of his childhood oath of enmity to Rome and is reconciled.","ideas":["Aetolian envoys canvass three kings to make war on Rome","Hannibal names Alexander, Pyrrhus and himself as the greatest generals","Rival claims over Smyrna, Lampsacus and the Asian Greek cities","Hannibal's oath sworn at the altar as a boy"],"terms":[{"t":"Apamea","d":"City where Villius first meets Antiochus before news of the king's son's death breaks off the talks."},{"t":"earth and water","d":"The Persian demand for submission, cited by the king's counsellors as a test made through small things."}]},{"i":245,"j":249,"loc":"Book XXXV 20–24","title":"Rome prepares for war with Antiochus","summary":"Rome assigns provinces, legions and fleets with Antiochus in view, and Atilius is sent with ships against Nabis. Prodigies are expiated, among them the consul Domitius's ox that is said to have spoken, and the Tiber floods again. Minucius defeats the Ligurians near Pisae, and Flaminius and Fulvius win towns in Spain, Fulvius taking Toletum. Rumours that Antiochus will strike Sicily lead to embassies to Greece, coastal defences and Baebius's army being moved toward Epirus. Attalus reports that Antiochus has crossed the Hellespont, and Publius Scipio Nasica and Manius Acilius are elected consuls.","ideas":["War expected but not yet declared","Prodigies, including the ox's warning to Rome","Sicily and the Adriatic coast guarded against invasion","Attalus brings word of the king's crossing"],"terms":[{"t":"tumultuary soldiers","d":"Troops raised in haste for an emergency outside the regular levy."}]},{"i":250,"j":256,"loc":"Book XXXV 25–30","title":"Philopoemen defeats Nabis in Laconia","summary":"The Achaeans, led by their praetor Philopoemen, declare war on Nabis for besieging Gythium. Philopoemen, unskilled at sea, loses an Achaean flagship that is an old and rotten quadrireme, but then burns Nabis's camp of huts at Pleiae in a night attack. Gythium falls, and the two armies meet near Lacedaemon, where Livy describes Philopoemen's habit of studying ground on every journey. An ambush at a rivulet and a feigned deserter lead Nabis to retreat; his army is routed on a narrow road and cut down at night outside the gates, leaving the tyrant shut in his city.","ideas":["The naval disaster of the rotten flagship","The burning of Nabis's camp at Pleiae","Philopoemen's study of terrain in peace and war","Ambush, false deserter and night interception"],"terms":[{"t":"Tarentines","d":"Horsemen of a type who rode with spare horses, used by both sides at the rivulet."},{"t":"targeteers","d":"Light infantry with small shields, hidden in a valley for the ambush."}]},{"i":257,"j":266,"loc":"Book XXXV 31–40","title":"Demetrias seized, Nabis murdered, Chalcis saved","summary":"Roman envoys tour the allies; at Demetrias the Magnetarch Eurylochus provokes Quinctius and flees to Aetolia. At the Panaetolian council the king's envoy Menippus and Thoas win over the multitude, and Damocritus scorns Quinctius, saying he will answer from the Tiber. The Aetolians then plot to seize Demetrias, Chalcis and Lacedaemon: Diocles takes Demetrias by a ruse, and Alexamenus kills Nabis during a review but is then killed by the Spartans. Philopoemen joins Lacedaemon to the Achaean League, while the Chalcidians, helped by Eretria and Carystus, foil Thoas. Livy notes that these Greek events are the causes of the war, then reports colonies, an earthquake and a fire at Rome.","ideas":["Aetolian decree inviting Antiochus to free Greece","Diocles takes Demetrias in the guise of an escort","Murder of Nabis and the Aetolians' ruin through plunder","Lacedaemon brought into the Achaean League"],"terms":[{"t":"Apocleti","d":"The select Aetolian council that devised the plots against the three cities."},{"t":"Magnetarch","d":"Chief magistrate of the Magnetians at Demetrias."}]},{"i":267,"j":277,"loc":"Book XXXV 41–51","title":"Antiochus crosses to Greece and takes Euboea","summary":"Rome allots provinces for the coming war. Thoas persuades Antiochus not to give Hannibal a command, and the king lands at Demetrias with only ten thousand foot and six elephants. At Lamia he promises the Aetolians future armies and is named their general, after Thoas overrides Phaeneas's call to use him as a mediator. Chalcis rebuffs him, but Amynander is won over by the claims of Philip of Megalopolis. At Aegium Quinctius mocks the king's boasted host, likening it to a feast of tame pork dressed as game, and the Achaeans declare war; Menippus then attacks Roman soldiers at Delium and Antiochus takes Chalcis and Euboea.","ideas":["Envy turns the king against using Hannibal","Antiochus arrives with a force too small for his promises","Quinctius's speech at Aegium and the Achaean declaration","Attack at Delium and the fall of Chalcis"],"terms":[{"t":"Cataphracti","d":"Mail-clad cavalry boasted of by the king's envoy."},{"t":"asylum","d":"Sanctuary status of the temple at Delium, where the Roman soldiers were attacked unprepared."}]},{"i":278,"j":288,"loc":"Book XXXVI 1–7","title":"War declared; Hannibal's plan for the war","summary":"The new consuls sacrifice and get favourable omens, and the people vote war on Antiochus and his adherents. Acilius draws Greece and vows great games to Jupiter, and the heralds rule that the Aetolians have already declared war on themselves. Rome declines gifts of money from Philip, Ptolemy, Carthage and Masinissa, accepting only grain paid for and ships owed by treaty. Antiochus winters at Chalcis, receives evasive Epirot envoys and wins a disguised decree from the Boeotians. At Demetrias Hannibal urges that Philip be brought in or kept busy, the whole force called from Asia, and Italy itself threatened.","ideas":["Formal declaration of war against Antiochus","Rome accepts only paid-for supplies from its allies","The Epirots hedge between king and Rome","Hannibal's strategy: win Philip and carry the war to Italy"],"terms":[{"t":"fetials (college of heralds)","d":"Priests consulted on the forms of declaring war."},{"t":"Lectisternium","d":"Feast offered to the gods, at whose shrines the consuls sacrificed before the war."}]},{"i":289,"j":296,"loc":"Book XXXVI 8–14","title":"Campaign in Thessaly and Acarnania","summary":"Hannibal's advice is praised but not followed. Antiochus buries the Macedonian bones at Cynoscephalae, angering Philip, who joins the Romans. The king takes Pherae, Scotussa and other towns, but Appius Claudius's ruse of many campfires near Gonni makes him lift the siege of Larissa. He then marries a young woman at Chalcis and spends the winter feasting while his army grows slack, and in spring wins Medio in Acarnania by treachery but is turned back at Thurium. Baebius and Philip retake Thessalian towns, Acilius arrives with his legions, Philip seizes Athamania as Amynander flees, and the consul advances toward the Malian bay.","ideas":["Bones at Cynoscephalae drive Philip to Rome's side","Campfire ruse relieves Larissa","The king's winter of marriage and revelry at Chalcis","Philip of Megalopolis mocked as king and brother"],"terms":[{"t":"Tempe","d":"Valley at whose mouth Gonni stands, where Appius camped."}]},{"i":297,"j":302,"loc":"Book XXXVI 15–21","title":"The battle of Thermopylae","summary":"Let down by the Aetolians, Antiochus fortifies himself inside the pass of Thermopylae, which Livy describes. Half the Aetolians hold the heights, the rest stay in Heraclea. Acilius sends Cato and Flaccus against the summits and tells his troops that victory will open Asia to Rome. The phalanx holds the rampart until Cato, having surprised the Aetolians on Callidromus, appears above the camp; the king's army panics and is pursued, and Antiochus flees to Chalcis and then Ephesus. Euboea submits, the army is praised for its moderation, and Cato races to Rome with the news.","ideas":["Description of the pass and its single road","Acilius's speech promising empire from Gades to the Red Sea","Cato's descent from Callidromus decides the battle","Polybius and Valerius Antias give differing numbers"],"terms":[{"t":"Sarissophori","d":"Macedonian pikemen whose long spears formed a second rampart."},{"t":"Thermopylae (Pylae)","d":"The pass named from its gate-like narrows and warm spring."}]},{"i":303,"j":311,"loc":"Book XXXVI 22–30","title":"Heraclea falls; the Aetolians surrender and resist","summary":"Acilius besieges Heraclea from four sides; the Aetolians defend by sallies until worn down by sleeplessness, and the consul breaks in by a timed pause and dawn assault, taking the citadel and Damocritus. Philip's siege of Lamia is called off so the Romans may take it. The Aetolians sue for peace and surrender to Roman faith, but when Acilius demands men be handed over and threatens Phaeneas with chains, Phaeneas protests that Greeks did not mean slavery. Nicander returns with money and promises from Antiochus, Philip treats him kindly, and Acilius marches over Oeta to besiege Naupactum.","ideas":["Siege of Heraclea won by exhausting the defenders","Surrender to Roman faith understood differently by the two sides","Nicander's report revives the will to fight","Sacrifice to Hercules at the Pyre on Oeta"],"terms":[{"t":"fides (faith)","d":"Surrender to Roman good faith, which the consul treats as unconditional submission."}]},{"i":312,"j":317,"loc":"Book XXXVI 31–35","title":"Quinctius settles Greece; the Aetolian truce","summary":"Quinctius stops the Achaean siege of Messene and orders Messene into the League. He wins Zacynthus back from the Achaeans, comparing them to a tortoise safe only inside its shell. Philip takes Demetrias, where Eurylochus kills himself, and other districts. At Naupactum Quinctius warns Acilius that Philip is gaining whole nations while the consul besieges two towns, and he arranges a truce so the Aetolians can send envoys to Rome. The Epirots receive pardon rather than acquittal, Philip's son Demetrius is returned to him, and Livy closes the war in Greece.","ideas":["Messene placed under Rome's settlement","The tortoise simile for the Peloponnese","Concern that Philip's power is growing too fast","Truce for the Aetolians to plead at Rome"],"terms":[{"t":"Achaean confederacy","d":"The league of Peloponnesian states that Messene, Elis and Lacedaemon are drawn into."}]},{"i":318,"j":322,"loc":"Book XXXVI 36–40","title":"Scipio Nasica's Boian war and triumph","summary":"The senate makes Publius Cornelius Scipio pay for his vowed games from spoils or his own purse, and the temples of the Idaean Mother and of Youth are dedicated. Prodigies, including oxen on a rooftop, are expiated and a fast to Ceres is instituted. Minucius beats off a Ligurian night attack, and Scipio wins a great victory over the Boians, though Livy distrusts Valerius Antias's figures. When the tribune Sempronius Blaesus proposes to postpone his triumph until Liguria is subdued, Scipio replies that Liguria was not his province and cites his naming as the best man in the state; he triumphs over the Boians.","ideas":["Vowed games paid without public money","Dedication of the temple of the Idaean Mother","Victory over the Boians and seizure of half their land","Dispute over a deferred triumph"],"terms":[{"t":"Megalesian games","d":"Games of the Great Mother, to which plays were first added at this dedication, per Antias."},{"t":"devoting law","d":"Solemn rite under which the Ligurians raised the army that attacked Minucius."}]},{"i":323,"j":326,"loc":"Book XXXVI 41–45","title":"The naval victory off Corycus","summary":"At Ephesus Antiochus feels secure, but Hannibal warns that the Romans will soon come to Asia by land and sea. Caius Livius gathers allied ships, joins the old fleet at Piraeus and is met by Eumenes. Polyxenidas urges battle before the Rhodians arrive, trusting his lighter ships. Off Corycus Livius grapples the enemy so as to fight as on land, the king's squadrons flee to Ephesus with thirteen ships taken and ten sunk, and the Romans winter at Cannae. Lucius Cornelius Scipio and Caius Laelius are elected consuls, expected to end the war.","ideas":["Hannibal foresees the war carried into Asia","Polyxenidas counts on speed and local knowledge","Grappling turns the sea fight into a land fight","The defeated fleet blockaded in Ephesus"],"terms":[{"t":"quinqueremes","d":"Warships with five banks of oars, the main Roman ship of the line."}]},{"i":327,"j":337,"loc":"Book XXXVII, summary and 1–7","title":"Scipio brothers take the war to Asia","summary":"The senate refuses the Ætolians peace unless they submit unconditionally or pay a thousand talents, and expels their envoys. When both consuls want Greece, Africanus offers to serve as lieutenant to his brother Lucius Scipio, and the province goes to him; prodigies are expiated and an eclipse occurs as the consul leaves. Acilius storms Lamia and besieges Amphissa until the Scipios arrive; through Athenian mediation and Africanus, the Ætolians win a six-month truce. Tiberius Gracchus rides to Pella to test Philip, finds roads and supplies ready, and Philip escorts the army through Macedon and Thrace to the Hellespont.","ideas":["Africanus serves as his brother's lieutenant, matching Hannibal's conqueror against Antiochus","Ætolian hopes rest on clemency, not on their cause","Lamia taken by surprise; Amphissa besieged by regular approaches","Philip's loyalty tested by a sudden visit and found sound"],"terms":[{"t":"legatus (lieutenant-general)","d":"A senior officer serving under a commander; Africanus took this lower rank under his brother."}]},{"i":338,"j":343,"loc":"Book XXXVII 8–13","title":"Polyxenidas destroys the Rhodian fleet at Panormus","summary":"Antiochus winters in Phrygia gathering forces and sends Hannibal to bring a Phœnician fleet. Livius receives Sestos and besieges Abydos, while Phocæa slides towards the king. Polyxenidas, a Rhodian exile, feigns treason to the Rhodian admiral Pausistratus, lulls him into dispersing his ships, then traps him in the harbour of Panormus by land and sea; Pausistratus is killed and only a few ships escape with fire-pots on their prows. Seleucus takes Phocæa by treachery, Livius abandons Abydos, and the allied fleet, after a storm, offers battle at Ephesus in vain.","ideas":["A forged written pledge of treachery deceives Pausistratus","Ambush at Panormus by land and sea","Fire carried on poles saves the escaping Rhodian ships","Rhodian grief turns to anger and a new fleet under Eudamus"],"terms":[]},{"i":344,"j":352,"loc":"Book XXXVII 14–21","title":"Councils at sea and the siege of Pergamus","summary":"Æmilius Regillus takes over the fleet; a council rejects Livius' plan of blocking Ephesus harbour with sunken ships after Eumenes shows its flaws, and Livius is sent to Lycia, where a bloody fight near Patara achieves nothing. Æmilius turns back from Jassus and Patara under criticism. Seleucus and Antiochus besiege Pergamus; Antiochus offers to negotiate, but Eumenes argues that no peace can be made without the consul and senate. A thousand Achæans under Diophanes, a pupil of Philopœmen, rout the careless besiegers twice, forcing Seleucus to withdraw.","ideas":["Eumenes' argument against blockading Ephesus","No treaty valid without consul, senate and people","Diophanes exploits the enemy's negligence before Pergamus"],"terms":[]},{"i":353,"j":357,"loc":"Book XXXVII 22–26","title":"Rhodians defeat Hannibal; Antiochus courts Prusias","summary":"A Rhodian squadron under Eudamus meets the king's Syrian fleet, commanded on one wing by Hannibal, off Side; after initial disorder, superior Rhodian seamanship routs it, though sickness among the rowers prevents a full pursuit. Antiochus writes to Prusias of Bithynia that Rome means to abolish all kingship, but letters from the Scipios, citing Masinissa and the kings Rome has left on their thrones, and a visit from Livius keep Prusias loyal. Antiochus then besieges Notium to draw the Roman fleet out to battle, and Eudamus persuades Æmilius to relieve the Colophonians rather than go to the Hellespont.","ideas":["Naval battle off Side: skill over numbers","Antiochus' warning of a spreading Roman conflagration","Africanus' reply: Rome enlarges allied kings"],"terms":[]},{"i":358,"j":363,"loc":"Book XXXVII 27–32","title":"The naval battle of Myonnesus and Phocæa","summary":"Seeking provisions, Æmilius threatens Teos, which had supplied the king's fleet; Polyxenidas waits at Macris to trap him as at Panormus, but the Romans move to the inner harbour. Warned by a peasant, the Romans and Rhodians embark in haste and meet the royal fleet between Myonnesus and Corycus; Rhodian fire-ships and Roman valour break the enemy, who lose forty-two ships. Antiochus in alarm abandons Lysimachia and the siege of Colophon and concentrates on a land battle. Æmilius besieges Phocæa, which surrenders; his soldiers plunder it against his orders, but he restores the citizens' city, lands and laws.","ideas":["Polyxenidas' repeated harbour-trap fails","Myonnesus ends royal command of the sea","Lysimachia abandoned, a mistake the event proves","Plunder of surrendered Phocæa against the commander's word"],"terms":[]},{"i":364,"j":369,"loc":"Book XXXVII 33–38","title":"Crossing to Asia and Scipio's answer","summary":"The consul finds Lysimachia stocked and abandoned and crosses the Hellespont unopposed, pausing for the festival of the Salian shields. Antiochus' envoy Heraclides offers half the war's cost and the disputed cities; the Romans demand the whole cost and evacuation of Asia west of Taurus. Privately offered his captured son and a share of the kingdom, Africanus refuses any public bargain and advises the king to accept terms. The son is returned to the sick Africanus, who advises Antiochus not to fight until he rejoins the army; the king fortifies a camp near Magnesia, and early cavalry skirmishes go the Romans' way.","ideas":["Scipio: a king who left the passage open must take orders","Private gratitude kept separate from public duty","Antiochus entrenches at Magnesia by Sipylus"],"terms":[{"t":"Salii","d":"Priests of Mars whose rite of moving the sacred shields made the period unfit for marching; Africanus was one of them."}]},{"i":370,"j":375,"loc":"Book XXXVII 39–44","title":"The battle of Magnesia","summary":"With winter near, the Roman council demands battle and Antiochus at last draws out his huge, mixed army: a deep phalanx with elephants, mailed cavalry, scythed chariots and dromedaries, against two legions and allies. A mist hampers the king's long line and slackens bowstrings; Eumenes' light troops stampede the chariots, which throw the king's own left into flight, and the phalanx is broken. Antiochus routs the Roman left by the river and nearly reaches the camp until the tribune Marcus Æmilius forces the fugitives back and Attalus arrives. The king's army is slaughtered, about fifty thousand foot by report, and Antiochus flees to Apamea.","ideas":["Scythed chariots turned against their own side","Mist and moisture favour the Romans' heavy arms","Marcus Æmilius rallies the fleeing left at the camp","Enormous royal losses against a few hundred Roman dead"],"terms":[{"t":"sarissa","d":"The long Macedonian pike, useless once the phalanx's ranks were broken."},{"t":"cataphracti (mailed horsemen)","d":"Cavalry with full armour on man and horse, too heavy to escape when routed."}]},{"i":376,"j":382,"loc":"Book XXXVII 45–51","title":"Terms for Antiochus; Ætolians spurned at Rome","summary":"Asian cities surrender and Antiochus' envoys beg pardon; Africanus answers that Rome's spirit is the same in good and bad fortune and repeats the earlier terms: give up Europe and Asia west of Taurus, pay fifteen thousand talents, and surrender Hannibal and Thoas. At Rome Acilius triumphs, a disaster in Spain is reported, colonists are sent to Placentia and Cremona, and Fulvius and Manlius are elected consuls. Livy notes but doubts Valerius Antias' story of the Scipios' capture. The Ætolian envoys offend the senate by boasting and are expelled; provinces are assigned and the pontiff overrules the flamen Fabius Pictor.","ideas":["Same terms offered to the conquered as before the battle","Hannibal's surrender demanded above all","Livy weighs a single doubtful source","Religious authority prevails over a prætor's province"],"terms":[{"t":"flamen Quirinalis","d":"Priest of Quirinus (Romulus), held by religious duty at Rome by the chief pontiff."}]},{"i":383,"j":387,"loc":"Book XXXVII 52–56","title":"Eumenes and the Rhodians before the senate","summary":"Eumenes, after modestly declining to name his reward, argues that Rome should hold the conquered lands or give them to its proven friends rather than free cities that had sided with Antiochus, citing his father's loyalty and comparing himself with Masinissa. The Rhodians reply that Rome fought for glory and Greek freedom, not territory, and that the Greek cities of Asia deserve liberty just as Marseilles keeps its Greek character; Eumenes can be rewarded with the rich inland conquests. The senate ratifies Scipio's peace and divides Asia: most of the former royal lands to Eumenes, Lycia and Caria beyond the Mæander to Rhodes, others free. Rhodes yields its request for Soli rather than disturb the peace.","ideas":["King's interest versus free cities' liberty","Rome as patron of Greek freedom","Ten commissioners to settle Asia"],"terms":[]},{"i":388,"j":392,"loc":"Book XXXVII 57–60","title":"Censorship contest and the triumphs over Antiochus","summary":"The prætor Bæbius dies after a Ligurian ambush, while Æmilius Paulus defeats the Lusitanians and a Latin colony is founded at Bononia. In the contest for the censorship, tribunes prosecute the popular Acilius Glabrio for withholding royal treasure, Cato testifying against him; Glabrio withdraws, and Flamininus and Marcellus are elected. Æmilius Regillus gains a naval triumph, and Lucius Scipio, taking the surname Asiaticus, holds a lavish triumph, which Livy judges greater in display than his brother's but not in difficulty. Fabius Labeo recovers captives from Crete.","ideas":["Cato's testimony against Glabrio","Asiaticus' surname matching Africanus","Antiochus' war not comparable with Hannibal's"],"terms":[]},{"i":393,"j":399,"loc":"Book XXXVIII, summary and 1–4","title":"Athamania revolts; Fulvius besieges Ambracia","summary":"Amynander, exiled king of Athamania, returns with Ætolian help as conspirators expel Philip's garrisons from four towns at once. Philip marches in, but retreats in near-flight from the hills, and his officer Zeno's detachment is destroyed at Ethopia. The Ætolians then take Amphilochia, Aperantia and Dolopia, but news of Antiochus' defeat and of the consul Fulvius' crossing dismays them. On Epirot advice Fulvius besieges Ambracia, enclosing it with camps and works; Eupolemus slips into the city while the Ætolian prætor Nicander turns to ravage Acarnania.","ideas":["Coordinated rising restores Amynander","Philip's retreat from Athamania","Ambracia's strong site between river and hill"],"terms":[]},{"i":400,"j":406,"loc":"Book XXXVIII 5–11","title":"Siege of Ambracia and the Ætolian peace","summary":"The Ambracians resist the rams with cranes, grapples and sallies, and a night attack by Nicodamus fails because Nicander never attacks from outside. The Romans dig a mine; the defenders countermine, fight underground and drive the miners out with smoke from burning feathers blown by bellows. Hard-pressed on three fronts, the Ætolians sue for peace; Amynander persuades Ambracia to surrender and its statues and paintings are carried off. At Rome, despite Philip's complaints, the Athenian Leon compares the Ætolian people to a calm sea stirred by agitators, and a treaty binds Ætolia to Rome's majesty, hostages and an indemnity.","ideas":["Mine and countermine with a smoke-cask","Nicander's failure to support the sortie","Ambracia stripped of Pyrrhus' artworks","Ætolians must respect Rome's empire and majesty"],"terms":[{"t":"maiestas populi Romani","d":"The dignity of the Roman people, which the treaty obliges the Ætolians to uphold."}]},{"i":407,"j":412,"loc":"Book XXXVIII 12–17","title":"Manlius marches against the Gallogræcians","summary":"The consul Cneius Manlius takes over Scipio's army and, with Attalus, marches through Caria, Pisidia and Phrygia, exacting money from towns and from Moagetes, the tyrant of Cibyra, who pleads poverty with false tears. Livy recounts how Gauls under Brennus' followers crossed into Asia, helped Nicomedes win Bithynia, divided Asia into three tributary zones among the Tolistoboians, Trocmians and Tectosagians, and were first defeated by Attalus. Manlius tells his soldiers that these Gauls are terrifying in show but tire quickly, and have degenerated in the soft Asian soil into Gallogræcians.","ideas":["Tribute levied on the march, e.g. Cibyra","Origin of the Galatian Gauls in Asia","Soil and climate change a people's nature","Warning against Asian pleasures"],"terms":[{"t":"Gallogræci","d":"The Gauls settled in Asia; Manlius calls them a mixed, degenerate race."}]},{"i":413,"j":418,"loc":"Book XXXVIII 18–23","title":"The battle on Mount Olympus","summary":"Manlius crosses the Sangarius, where priests of the Great Mother from Pessinus foretell victory, and finds Gordium deserted; the Gauls withdraw with families and goods to Mounts Olympus and Magaba. Expecting a fight at a distance, he stocks huge quantities of missiles and attacks Olympus in three columns. The Gauls, naked and with poor shields, are shot down by the light troops and driven into their camp, then flee over cliffs. Thousands die and about forty thousand are captured; Livy notes the conflicting totals of Claudius and Valerius Antias, and Attalus is praised.","ideas":["Prophecy at the Sangarius","Missile warfare against men eager for close combat","Gallic flight over the precipices","Disputed casualty figures"],"terms":[{"t":"velites (light infantry)","d":"Skirmishers with javelins, a short shield and a Spanish sword, decisive on Olympus."}]},{"i":419,"j":422,"loc":"Book XXXVIII 24–27","title":"Ortiagon's wife and the defeat of the Tectosagians","summary":"At Ancyra the captive wife of the chieftain Ortiagon, violated by a centurion who then sells her ransom, has him killed as he weighs the gold and carries his head to her husband. The Tectosagians stall with talk of a conference and set an ambush of a thousand horse for the consul, which is beaten off when a foraging guard arrives. Manlius then attacks Mount Magaba, where the same missile tactics break the Gauls, who flee across the Halys. Beaten, they sue for peace, and Manlius winters on the coast.","ideas":["A captive's revenge for her violated chastity","Treachery at a conference punished","Second mountain battle repeats the first"],"terms":[]},{"i":423,"j":429,"loc":"Book XXXVIII 28–34","title":"Same falls; the Achæans subdue Sparta","summary":"At Rome the censors name Africanus chief of the senate and the Tiber floods twelve times. In Cephallenia Same revolts, resists for four months against slingers from Ægium, and is finally sold into slavery. In Peloponnese Philopœmen moves the Achæan assembly from Ægium to Argos and, after Sparta seizes the coastal village of Las, demands its ringleaders; Sparta kills his partisans and offers itself to Rome. The senate's ambiguous answer emboldens the Achæans: exiles stone seventeen Spartans and sixty-three more are condemned, the walls are pulled down and the laws of Lycurgus abolished.","ideas":["Achæan slingers trained on beach pebbles","Philopœmen's hostility to Sparta and support for the exiles","End of the discipline of Lycurgus after seven hundred years"],"terms":[{"t":"princeps senatus","d":"The senator listed first on the roll; Africanus was named a third time."}]},{"i":430,"j":434,"loc":"Book XXXVIII 35–39","title":"The treaty of Apamea and the settlement of Asia","summary":"Fulvius holds elections, keeping out his enemy Lepidus; the Campanians gain marriage rights, and Formiæ, Fundi and Arpinum receive the vote. In Asia the allies rejoice more at the Gauls' defeat than at Antiochus', and Ariarathes buys pardon. Manlius and the ten commissioners conclude the treaty with Antiochus: evacuate Asia to the Halys and Taurus, give up elephants and warships, pay twelve thousand talents over twelve years, give hostages and surrender Hannibal. Antiochus' ships at Patara are burned, and lands, freedom and tribute are distributed, chiefly to Eumenes and Rhodes, with Ilium favoured for Rome's origin.","ideas":["Terms of the peace with Antiochus","Freedom for loyal cities, tribute to Eumenes for others","Respect for Trojan origins in the awards"],"terms":[]},{"i":435,"j":440,"loc":"Book XXXVIII 40–44","title":"Thracian ambush and quarrels at Rome","summary":"Returning loaded with booty through Thrace, Manlius' army is attacked in a wooded defile by about ten thousand Thracians, some suspecting Philip's connivance; baggage is lost and Quintus Minucius Thermus is killed before a second attack is beaten in open ground. At Rome the new consul Lepidus objects that Fulvius and Manlius rule abroad like kings while consuls are kept in Liguria, and both are recalled. Lepidus brings in Ambracian envoys to accuse Fulvius of plundering their temples; Flaminius defends him, but in his absence the senate restores the Ambracians' property and liberty. Manlius returns and asks for a triumph, opposed by most of the ten commissioners.","ideas":["The army's booty invites the Thracian attack","Death of Minucius Thermus","Personal enmity drives the charges against Fulvius"],"terms":[]},{"i":441,"j":446,"loc":"Book XXXVIII 45–49","title":"Debate over Manlius' triumph","summary":"Furius and Æmilius Paulus charge that Manlius tried to wreck the peace, crossed Taurus against the Sibylline warning, and attacked the Gauls with no decree of senate or people, wandering for plunder as Attalus' hireling. They add that he fought on bad ground, won only because the Gauls were degenerate, and was then beaten by Thracian robbers. Manlius replies that the Gauls oppressed Asia and had fought in Antiochus' army, so they were already enemies, and that the war secured Rome's settlement up to Taurus. He defends his choice of ground and claims the Thracian fight was itself a victory.","ideas":["War without senate decree or heralds' rites","The Gauls as Antiochus' allies and Asia's oppressors","Envy depreciates merit, says Manlius"],"terms":[{"t":"fetiales (heralds)","d":"Priests whose rites declared war; the accusers say Manlius ignored them."}]},{"i":447,"j":451,"loc":"Book XXXVIII 50–54","title":"Africanus prosecuted, withdraws to Liternum, and dies","summary":"The senate grants Cneius Manlius his triumph, but attention shifts to the prosecution of Publius Scipio Africanus by the two Petillii. Instead of answering the charges, Scipio praises his own deeds, and on the anniversary of Zama he leads the whole assembly to the Capitol to thank the gods. He then retires to Liternum and refuses to attend; the tribune Tiberius Gracchus, though his enemy, vetoes the trial as a disgrace to Rome. Livy sums up Scipio as greater in war than in peace and records his wish to be buried away from his ungrateful country. After his death, with Cato's backing, the Petillian motion for an inquiry into the money taken from Antiochus is carried.","ideas":["Debate over whether the greatest citizen should answer to the laws","Scipio's procession to the Capitol on the anniversary of Zama","Gracchus sets aside private enmity to protect Africanus","Cato's influence pushes through the Petillian inquiry"],"terms":[{"t":"tribunus plebis (plebeian tribune)","d":"Officer of the commons who could prosecute before the people and veto acts of other magistrates."},{"t":"Petillian order","d":"The people's vote, moved by the Petillii, setting up an inquiry into unaccounted money from Antiochus."}]},{"i":452,"j":458,"loc":"Book XXXVIII 55–60","title":"Lucius Scipio convicted and the conflicting traditions","summary":"Before the praetor Quintus Terentius Culleo, Lucius Scipio, his legate Aulus Hostilius and his quaestor Furius are convicted of taking bribes from Antiochus; Livy suspects a copying error in Antias's sums. He then sets out the disagreements among his sources over Africanus's accuser, death, and burial, and the story of how the younger daughter came to be betrothed to Gracchus. When Lucius is ordered to prison, Publius Scipio Nasica defends the family and the peace terms in a long speech. Gracchus alone of the tribunes forbids his imprisonment; the sale of Lucius's goods does not even cover the fine, and public anger turns on the praetor and the accusers.","ideas":["Livy weighs contradictory accounts of Scipio's trial and death","Nasica argues the peace with Antiochus took more than any bribe could buy","Gracchus's decree frees Lucius from chains","Lucius's estate proves too small to have held royal treasure"],"terms":[{"t":"peculatus (embezzlement)","d":"Misappropriation of public money, the charge brought against Lucius Scipio and his staff."},{"t":"Cornelian family","d":"The patrician clan (gens) to which the Scipios belonged."}]},{"i":459,"j":470,"loc":"Book XXXIX summary, 1–7","title":"Ligurian wars, Fulvius's triumph, and Asiatic luxury","summary":"After the book's summary, Livy praises Liguria as a hard school for soldiers, in contrast to soft and wealthy Asia. The consuls Flaminius and Aemilius subdue Ligurian tribes and build roads; a praetor who disarmed the Cenomani is sent away, and twelve thousand Latins are returned to their towns. Marcus Fulvius wins his Aetolian triumph against the opposition of the tribune Abutius, whom Tiberius Gracchus shames for acting as another man's agent. Cneius Manlius triumphs late to avoid prosecution, and Livy dates the start of foreign luxury at Rome to his returning army.","ideas":["Liguria as the proving ground of Roman discipline","Gracchus rebukes a tribune for serving a consul's private quarrel","Triumph lists of Fulvius and Manlius","Couches, cooks and harp-girls as the seeds of luxury"],"terms":[{"t":"triumphus (triumph)","d":"The victory procession into Rome granted by the senate to a successful commander."},{"t":"luxuria (luxury)","d":"Extravagant living, which Livy traces to the army returning from Asia."}]},{"i":471,"j":478,"loc":"Book XXXIX 8–14","title":"The Bacchanalian rites discovered through Aebutius and Hispala","summary":"Livy describes how a Greek priest of nocturnal rites brought the Bacchanalia from Etruria to Rome, where wine and darkness led to vice, forgery and murder. The young Publius Aebutius is to be initiated by his mother and stepfather, who want to ruin him, but his lover, the freedwoman Hispala Fecenia, warns him off. Turned out of the house, he informs the consul Postumius, who questions his aunt and then, in his mother-in-law Sulpicia's house, draws a full account from the terrified Hispala. She tells how Paculla Minia changed the rites by admitting men and holding them by night, and how those who resisted were killed. Postumius lodges both informers safely.","ideas":["The cult's spread from Etruria to Rome","Hispala's warning and Aebutius's refusal","Postumius's careful private inquiry","Paculla Minia's changes to the rites"],"terms":[{"t":"Bacchanalia","d":"Secret rites of Bacchus, here described as nocturnal gatherings of men and women given to crime."},{"t":"libertina (freedwoman)","d":"A woman freed from slavery, as Hispala Fecenia was."}]},{"i":479,"j":484,"loc":"Book XXXIX 14–19","title":"The senate's suppression of the Bacchanalia","summary":"The senate orders an extraordinary inquiry, rewards for informers, and a ban on Bacchanalian gatherings throughout Italy. In an address to the people a consul calls the cult a conspiracy against the state and defends the suppression of foreign rites as true piety. Panic spreads; more than seven thousand are said to have sworn the oath, and the leaders are seized. Those merely initiated are imprisoned, those guilty of crimes executed, and women handed to their families for punishment. Future rites need senate approval and are limited to five people, and Aebutius and Hispala receive money and privileges.","ideas":["Consul's speech: foreign rites as a threat to Roman religion","Trials held in country towns as suspects flee","Distinction between the merely initiated and the criminal","Strict limits on any future Bacchic worship"],"terms":[{"t":"quaestio extraordinaria (special inquiry)","d":"A court set up by the senate outside the normal course to try a particular matter."},{"t":"senatus consultum (decree of the senate)","d":"A formal resolution of the senate, here regulating the rites."}]},{"i":485,"j":489,"loc":"Book XXXIX 20–23","title":"Defeat at the Marcian pass and Philip's grievances","summary":"The consul Quintus Marcius is trapped by the Apuan Ligurians and loses four thousand men in a defile afterwards named the Marcian pass. From Spain come news of Atinius's victory and death and of Manlius's defeat of the Celtiberians at Calaguris. Fulvius's games show Rome its first wrestling contests and a lion hunt, prodigies are expiated, and Transalpine Gauls settle near the future Aquileia. Livy then explains that the war with Perseus began with Philip, who was angered at being denied his revolted subjects and recalled from Lamia, but who gained Athamania, Demetrias, and cities in Thrace.","ideas":["The Marcian pass disaster","Victories and losses in both Spains","Lavish votive games of Fulvius and Lucius Scipio","Philip's grievances as the true root of the Macedonian war"],"terms":[{"t":"ludi votivi (votive games)","d":"Games a commander vowed in war and later held to fulfil his vow."}]},{"i":490,"j":496,"loc":"Book XXXIX 24–29","title":"Philip answers his accusers at Tempe and Thessalonica","summary":"Philip quietly builds up his kingdom's revenue and population, while Thessalians, Perrhaebians, Athamanians, Maronites and Eumenes complain at Rome about his seizures. At Tempe, before the Roman envoys led by Caecilius Metellus, the Thessalians accuse him of plundering their towns and diverting their trade. Philip replies with counter-charges and the threatening remark that his sun has not yet set; the envoys order his garrisons withdrawn. At Thessalonica, about Aenus and Maronea, Philip attacks Roman ingratitude for his help against Antiochus, and the envoys refer the matter to the senate. The section closes with Manlius's ovation and the suppression of a slave rising in Apulia.","ideas":["Philip's preparation for a future war","The Thessalians' complaints and Philip's menacing retort","Philip's speech on Roman ingratitude","Rulings that limit Macedonia to its old frontiers"],"terms":[{"t":"ovatio (ovation)","d":"A lesser triumph, granted to Manlius because he had not handed over a settled province."}]},{"i":497,"j":499,"loc":"Book XXXIX 30–32","title":"Victory on the Tagus and a contested election","summary":"The praetors Calpurnius and Quintius are first routed near Toletum, then cross the Tagus and destroy a Spanish army of thirty-five thousand. Calpurnius leads the cavalry into the enemy's wedge, the centre legions break through, and the camp is stormed. In Liguria Sempronius and Appius Claudius win successes. At Rome the consul Appius Claudius openly canvasses for his brother Publius against the protests of the senate and so gets him elected consul.","ideas":["Defeat followed by revenge on the Tagus","Calpurnius's personal charge breaks the Spanish wedge","Appius Claudius's improper canvassing for his brother"],"terms":[{"t":"cuneus (wedge)","d":"A dense, wedge-shaped attacking column used by the Spaniards."}]},{"i":500,"j":504,"loc":"Book XXXIX 33–37","title":"Massacre at Maronea and the Achaean defence","summary":"A new embassy under Appius Claudius orders Philip out of Aenus, Maronea and the Thracian coast. In revenge Philip has Onomastus and Cassander massacre his opponents at Maronea, denies it, and is believed to have had Cassander poisoned. Planning war but needing time, he sends Demetrius to Rome. At Clitor, Appius charges the Achaeans with their treatment of Sparta; the praetor Lycortas replies that Spartans killed Spartans, that tearing down the tyrants' walls served Lycurgus's discipline, and that the Achaeans fear the gods more than Rome. Appius warns them to comply willingly, and only the death sentence on Areus and Alcibiades is reversed.","ideas":["Philip's massacre at Maronea and its cover-up","Demetrius sent to Rome to gain time","Lycortas's speech on equal alliance and Sparta's walls","Appius's veiled threat to the Achaeans"],"terms":[{"t":"Achaean council","d":"The federal assembly of the Achaean League in the Peloponnese."},{"t":"discipline of Lycurgus","d":"Sparta's ancient laws and way of life, said to have been abolished by the Achaeans."}]},{"i":505,"j":511,"loc":"Book XXXIX 38–44","title":"Cato's censorship and the expulsion of Flamininus","summary":"After disputes over the Spanish armies and over Quintus Fulvius Flaccus seeking the praetorship while aedile-elect, which the senate blocks, a fierce contest is fought for the censorship. Livy praises Marcus Porcius Cato as versatile, austere and incorruptible; despite the nobles he is elected with Lucius Valerius Flaccus. Inquiries into poisoning and the last Bacchanalians go on. The censors expel seven senators, among them Lucius Quinctius Flamininus, for killing a Boian deserter at a banquet to please his favourite (Antias tells another version). Cato also degrades Lucius Scipio, taxes luxury heavily, and enforces harsh public contracts.","ideas":["Senate refuses to let one man stand for two curule offices","Livy's portrait of Cato","Flamininus's banquet killing in Cato's and Antias's versions","Cato's taxes on luxury and public works"],"terms":[{"t":"censor","d":"Magistrate who revised the rolls of senate and knights, assessed property, and let public contracts."},{"t":"nota censoria (censor's mark)","d":"The written reason a censor attached to a citizen's name when degrading him."}]},{"i":512,"j":517,"loc":"Book XXXIX 45–50","title":"Demetrius before the senate and Philopoemen's death","summary":"New consuls take office, and Transalpine Gauls building a town near Aquileia are to be stopped. Embassies from every neighbour of Macedonia crowd the senate with complaints against Philip; the young Demetrius, embarrassed, reads his father's replies, and the senate pardons Philip for his son's sake. Livy notes that this honour brought Demetrius envy and ruin. Sparta is kept in the Achaean League and its exiles restored. In the Messenian war the aged Philopoemen is captured shielding his horsemen, held in an underground treasury, and made to drink poison, asking first whether Lycortas was safe.","ideas":["The senate's favour to Demetrius as the cause of his ruin","Livy limits himself to foreign affairs tied to Rome","Philopoemen's capture and death","Philopoemen, Hannibal and Scipio said to die in one year"],"terms":[{"t":"obses (hostage)","d":"A person held as a pledge of a treaty, as Demetrius had been at Rome."}]},{"i":518,"j":523,"loc":"Book XXXIX 51–56","title":"Hannibal's death and the rivalry in Macedon","summary":"Flamininus visits Prusias, who has soldiers surround Hannibal's house; Hannibal takes poison, reproaching Rome for plotting a guest's murder. Livy argues from the princeps senatus list and Naevius's tribunate about the year of Scipio's death and compares the unworthy deaths of the three great men. In Macedon the people favour Demetrius as heir, which angers Perseus and Philip, and Philip campaigns in Thrace and founds Perseis. The Gauls near Aquileia are disarmed and sent home, colonies are planted, the Celtiberians are beaten, and elections and prodigies close the book.","ideas":["Hannibal's last words and suicide","Livy's reasoning about the date of Scipio's death","Demetrius's popularity arouses Perseus's fear","Senate warns that the Alps are a barrier"],"terms":[{"t":"princeps senatus (leader of the senate)","d":"The senator first on the censors' roll, whose appointment Livy uses to date Scipio's death."}]},{"i":524,"j":533,"loc":"Book XL summary, 1–7","title":"Philip's cruelty, Theoxena, and the brothers' quarrel","summary":"After the summary, provinces are assigned and storms and prodigies are expiated. Marcius reports that Philip is preparing for war; Philip moves the coastal population inland and resolves to seize the children of men he has killed. Theoxena, rather than surrender her family, gives the children a sword and a poison cup and leaps into the sea with her husband Poris. Perseus wins over his father's friends against Demetrius and turns Philip against him as a Roman spy. A mock battle at the army's purification grows bitter, and when Demetrius's party comes armed to Perseus's door after a feast, Perseus shuts them out as assassins.","ideas":["Forced resettlement of the coastal Macedonians","Theoxena's choice of death for her children","Perseus's plot to paint Demetrius as Rome's man","Purification rite, mock battle, and the night at Perseus's door"],"terms":[{"t":"lustratio (purification)","d":"The Macedonian army's ceremony of marching between the halves of a sacrificed dog."}]},{"i":534,"j":537,"loc":"Book XL 8–11","title":"Philip judges his sons; Perseus accuses Demetrius","summary":"Perseus tells Philip that Demetrius came by night with armed men to kill him. Philip, calling himself the most unhappy of fathers, holds a hearing and reproaches both sons with examples of brotherly concord, among them Eumenes and Attalus and the Scipios. Perseus describes the mock battle, the invitation to supper and the armed visit as one day of attempts on his life. He claims Rome, Flamininus and the Macedonian nobles back Demetrius and plot to make him king, and he presents himself as having no refuge but his father.","ideas":["Philip's speech on harmony between brothers","Perseus reads the night's events as attempted fratricide","Perseus claims Demetrius serves Roman interests","Perseus appeals to his rights as elder son"],"terms":[]},{"i":538,"j":542,"loc":"Book XL 12–15","title":"Demetrius's defence and Philip's suspended verdict","summary":"Demetrius, weeping, answers that Perseus's charges contradict each other: if Rome could make him king, he had no need of murder. He shows the absurdity of plotting murder at a purification, a supper and a revel all in one day, and says his drunken friends carried swords only for fear of revenge for the beaten spy. His real offence, he argues, is the people's favour, and his friendship with Rome came from missions his father sent him on. Philip refuses to judge after one hour; the night's charge is seen as refuted, but the suspicion about Rome remains, and Livy calls it a seed of the war with Perseus.","ideas":["Demetrius exposes contradictions in the accusation","Friendship with Rome as a pledge of peace, not treason","Envy, not crime, as Perseus's motive","Philip's lingering suspicion"],"terms":[]},{"i":543,"j":547,"loc":"Book XL 16–20","title":"Spain, Masinissa, plague, and spies at Rome","summary":"Ligurians surrender to Marcellus, Fulvius Flaccus takes Urbicua in Hither Spain, and Terentius enters Rome in ovation. Roman arbiters refer the land dispute between Carthage and Masinissa to the senate. New magistrates are elected, a fleet is formed against pirates, and a severe plague cuts short the levies while the inquiry into the Bacchanalians is renewed in Apulia. Philip's envoys Philocles and Apelles come to Rome to look into Demetrius's dealings with Flamininus, but they are Perseus's agents; Demetrius grows cautious and avoids the Romans.","ideas":["Siege of Urbicua","Carthage and Masinissa dispute the same land","Pestilence and prodigies at Rome","Philip's envoys secretly serve Perseus"],"terms":[{"t":"duumviri navales (naval commissioners)","d":"Two officers appointed to fit out and command a fleet guarding the coast."}]},{"i":548,"j":551,"loc":"Book XL 21–24","title":"Mount Haemus and the murder of Demetrius","summary":"Philip climbs Mount Haemus, hoping to view the seas, the Danube and the Alps in planning war on Rome, and sends Demetrius home under Didas, who is Perseus's man. The climb through forest and fog shows nothing, and the army plunders allied land and fails at Petra. Didas learns that Demetrius means to flee to Rome and betrays him; Philip's envoys return with a forged letter from Flamininus. Herodotus dies under torture without confessing, and Philip has Didas poison Demetrius at Heraclea, where he is finally smothered.","ideas":["Philip's futile ascent of Haemus","Didas wins and betrays Demetrius's trust","Forged letter of Titus Quinctius","Demetrius poisoned and smothered"],"terms":[]},{"i":552,"j":556,"loc":"Book XL 25–29","title":"Paullus's breakout and the books of Numa","summary":"The Ingaunian Ligurians ask Lucius Aemilius Paullus for a truce and use it to besiege his camp. Relief from Rome is slow because of the plague and a war with the Istrians, so Paullus harangues his men and sallies from all four gates against the careless enemy, killing over fifteen thousand. The Ingauni surrender and pirate ships are seized. During a long drought, two stone chests inscribed with Numa's name are dug up on Lucius Petillius's land; the body chest is empty and the other holds Latin books on pontifical law and Greek philosophical books. The praetor judges them a danger to religion and they are burned in the comitium.","ideas":["Ligurian treachery during a truce","Sally from every gate at once","Discovery of the books of Numa","Antias's claim of Pythagorean doctrine treated as fiction"],"terms":[{"t":"comitium","d":"The place of assembly in the Roman forum where Numa's books were burned."},{"t":"ius pontificium (pontifical law)","d":"The body of sacred law administered by the pontiffs."}]},{"i":557,"j":561,"loc":"Book XL 30–34","title":"Flaccus defeats the Celtiberians; Aquileia founded","summary":"Quintus Fulvius Flaccus, facing thirty-five thousand Celtiberians near Aebura, waits until they think him inactive, then sends Acilius round a mountain to seize their camp while he draws their army out and attacks. The burning camp dismays them and twenty-three thousand fall. At Contrebia rain delays the relieving force, which arrives in disorder and is routed, and most of Celtiberia submits. The Latin colony of Aquileia is founded, temples to Venus Erycina and Piety are dedicated, Paullus triumphs over the Ingauni, and Corsica and Sardinia see fighting.","ideas":["Feigned inactivity and a flank march take the enemy camp","Celtiberians routed at Contrebia","Founding of Aquileia","Senate distrusts the Ligurians' promises of peace"],"terms":[{"t":"colonia Latina (Latin colony)","d":"A settlement whose colonists held Latin rather than full Roman rights."}]},{"i":562,"j":566,"loc":"Book XL 35–39","title":"Veterans, poisoning, and the Apuani deported","summary":"Flaccus's envoys report Celtiberia subdued and ask that his restless veterans be brought home; his successor Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus argues that a province still in arms needs seasoned troops. The senate sends new levies and allows the long-serving men to return. The plague kills a praetor and the consul Piso, and Piso's wife Hostilia is convicted of poisoning him to make her son consul. Forty thousand Apuan Ligurians are moved to Samnium, and Cornelius and Baebius triumph without having fought. In Spain the Celtiberians ambush Flaccus's army in the Manlian pass as it marches to Tarraco.","ideas":["Debate over veteran versus new armies for Spain","Hostilia's prediction and conviction","Mass resettlement of the Apuan Ligurians","First triumph without a battle"],"terms":[]},{"i":567,"j":573,"loc":"Book XL 40–45","title":"Battle in the pass and the year's closing events","summary":"In the Manlian pass the Celtiberian wedge nearly breaks the legions until Flaccus orders the cavalry to charge it with bridles off; seventeen thousand of the enemy fall and he vows a temple to Equestrian Fortune. He hands over to Gracchus at Tarraco. In Liguria more tribes surrender, and Marcus Fulvius Nobilior is banished for disbanding a legion without authority. Duronius blames King Gentius for Adriatic piracy, plague kills many priests, and the Villian law fixes ages for office. Flaccus triumphs and is made consul, his games are held under a spending limit, and after a hard winter Lepidus and Nobilior, open enemies, are elected censors.","ideas":["Bridleless cavalry charge breaks the wedge","Punishment for illegally disbanding a legion","Gentius accused of piracy","Villian law on the ages for magistracies"],"terms":[{"t":"lex annalis (law of ages)","d":"The Villian law fixing at what ages each office could be sought."},{"t":"Fortuna Equestris (Equestrian Fortune)","d":"The goddess to whom Flaccus vowed a temple after the cavalry's success."}]},{"i":574,"j":581,"loc":"Book XL 46–53","title":"Censors reconciled; Gracchus subdues Celtiberia","summary":"Quintus Cæcilius Metellus urges the newly elected censors, Marcus Æmilius Lepidus and Marcus Fulvius Nobilior, to end their long feud, citing Romulus and Tatius and the proverb that enmities should be mortal; the two air their grievances and then publicly pledge friendship. In Spain the proprætor Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus takes Munda and Certima, routs the Celtiberians at Alce by a feigned flight, receives the submission of over a hundred towns and wins over the chieftain Thurrus; Livy notes a variant account of a hard battle near Mount Caunus. The reconciled censors revise the senate and let contracts for many public works, and Æmilius dedicates temples, including one to the sea gods vowed by Lucius Æmilius Regillus. The consul Quintus Fulvius defeats the Ligurians, while 3,000 Transalpine Gauls seeking land are ordered out of Italy.","ideas":["Metellus's appeal reconciles two hostile censors","Gracchus's feigned retreat lures the Celtiberians from camp at Alce","Thurrus, the most powerful Spaniard, joins Rome","Censors' building programme and new arrangement of the tribes"],"terms":[{"t":"censor","d":"Magistrate who reviewed the senate and knights, held the census, closed the lustrum and contracted for public works."},{"t":"lustrum","d":"The purificatory sacrifice that closed the censors' census."},{"t":"princeps senatus (head of the senate)","d":"The senator placed first on the roll, here Lepidus."}]},{"i":582,"j":587,"loc":"Book XL 54–59","title":"Death of Philip and accession of Perseus","summary":"Philip of Macedon, grieving for Demetrius and remorseful for his cruelty, is helped by the loyal Antigonus to uncover the plot: Xychus, threatened with torture, reveals that Apelles and Philocles forged the Flamininus letter that doomed Demetrius. Philip resolves to make Antigonus his heir instead of Perseus, but falls ill at Amphipolis, tormented by visions of his murdered son, and dies; the physician Calligenes alerts Perseus, who seizes the throne, kills Antigonus and asks Rome to renew the treaty. Philip's plan for the Bastarnians to destroy the Dardanians and invade Italy collapses: a storm and Thracian attacks on Mount Donuca scatter them, and only about 30,000 under Clondicus reach Dardania. At Rome Fulvius triumphs over the Ligurians, new consuls and prætors are elected, and prodigies are expiated by repeating the games.","ideas":["The forged letter behind Demetrius's death is exposed","Philip's failed attempt to pass the throne to Antigonus","Perseus seizes the kingdom by a secret warning of the death","The Bastarnian scheme against Dardania and Italy breaks down"],"terms":[{"t":"Bastarnians","d":"A people from beyond the Danube whom Philip invited south as a weapon against the Dardanians and Rome."},{"t":"lectisternium","d":"A rite in which images of the gods were set on couches before a feast; here disturbed by an earthquake."}]}]});